    "result_text": "All 279 tests pass, including the new `Cp18Tests`, with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field on owner creation, following the existing \"assign derived field\" pattern used by sibling steps like `AssignNamesakeCount` and `AssignSharesHousehold`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`AssignLocality.java`** (new) \u2014 A pipeline function that, before the owner is saved, tallies the cities of all existing owners (`OwnerRepository.findAll()`), finds the top count, and sets `locality` to `\"local\"` only when the owner's city is the *strictly* single most common existing city (no other city ties the top count); otherwise `\"remote\"`.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `@Transient String locality` field with getter/setter, matching how the other computed fields (`namesakeCount`, `sharesHousehold`, etc.) are held in-memory and returned through the same-transaction GET.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `locality` string property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field and MapStruct auto-maps it by name (no mapper change needed).\n\n4. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 Wired `assignLocality` into the creation pipeline after `assignSharesHousehold` and before `save`, so it computes against existing owners only (excluding the one being created).\n\n**Semantics note:** \"the *single* most common city\" is treated strictly \u2014 if two or more cities tie for the highest count, no city is the single most common, so the result is `\"remote\"`. This matches the test, where a hub city with 6 owners is unambiguously the most common (seed cities have only a few each).",
